<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="problem-alternative-characterization-of-uniform-convergence" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Alternative characterization of uniform convergence
  </title>
  
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m> \of{ f_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be a sequence of maps <m> f_{ n } \colon X \to Y </m> from a non-empty set <m> X \neq \emptyset </m> to a metric space <m> \of{ Y , \rho } </m>. Prove that <m> \of{ f_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges to a map <m> f \colon X \to Y </m> from <m> X </m> to <m> Y </m> if and only if for all positive real numbers <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m>, there is a natural number <m> n_{ \epsilon } \in \N </m> so that
      
      <md>
        0 \leq \sup_{ x \in X }{ \rho \of{ f_{ n } \of{ x } , f \of{ x } } } \lt \epsilon
      </md>
      
      for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  
  <solution>
    <p>
      We observe that for a given index <m> n \in \N </m>,
      
      <md>
        0 \leq \sup_{ x \in X }{ \rho \of{ f_{ n } \of{ x } , f \of{ x } } } \lt \epsilon
      </md>
      
      if and only if
      
      <md>
        \rho \of{ f_{ n } \of{ x } , f \of{ x } } \lt \epsilon
      </md>
      
      for all inputs <m> x \in X </m>. Thus the sequence <m> \of{ f_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges uniformly to <m> f </m> if and only if there is a natural number <m> n_{ \epsilon } \in \N </m> so that either (and therefore both) of the above conditions holds for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>
